  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington delivered solid momentum yesterday\u2014up 5% in total revenue on normalized capacity, with core grooming +7% and add-ons +55% attach rate. Year-to-date you're tracking +46% against last year on both revenue and appointments, a structural win. MTD performance is +12.6% above the DOW-adjusted expectation, meaning the team is outperforming even after calendar headwinds. Retail did dip 8%, but that's a minor offset to a strong grooming day. Bather hours ran hot at 6.3h vs. the 4.9h baseline\u2014leverage that capacity signal.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday was down 20% total, with grooming revenue off 25% and transactions down 35%. The DOW-adjusted lens shows you're only +3.6% against expectation MTD, which means there's real operational softness here, not calendar cover. Core grooms fell from $1,300 last Wednesday to $960\u2014that's a 26% week-over-week drop. Add-on attach collapsed from 36% to 22%, signaling upsell discipline has slipped. Groomer productivity sits at $342/day vs. your $415 baseline, and you're running 1.8 groomer-days per day against the prior month's 2.4. This isn't a holiday effect; capacity is contracted and the team isn't compensating with ticket lift.\n\nThe pattern across both stores: Port Washington is scaling (6 active groomers, strong bather hours, +39% appointments MTD), while Hicksville is contracting (3 active yesterday, zero bathers, -24% appointments MTD). Port has 469 lapsed customers worth $296k in annual value\u2014reactivation there is your biggest lever. Hicksville's 119 lapsed at $91k is meaningful but smaller. Inventory stress is real at both locations; X-Small Treat Table is critically short at Hicksville (-142 units).\n\nToday: Port Washington has 6 groomers and 7.0h bather coverage; Hicksville has 4 groomers and 0 bather hours. Next seven days: 52 total groomer-days (34 Port, 18 Hicksville) with 43 bather hours concentrated at Port Washington."
